## The four patterns

```jsx
// 1. Ternary — either/or
{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <LoginForm />}

// 2. && — show or render nothing
{hasError && <ErrorBanner />}

// 3. Early return — whole-component branch
function Profile({ user }) {
  if (!user) return <Spinner />;
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}

// 4. Lookup object — many branches, no nested ternaries
const views = { list: <ListView />, grid: <GridView />, map: <MapView /> };
return views[mode] ?? <ListView />;
```

---

## The && trap

```jsx
// ❌ Renders a literal "0" on the page when the cart is empty
{cart.length && <CartSummary items={cart} />}
```

`0 && anything` evaluates to `0`. React skips `false`, `null` and `undefined`, but **`0` is a valid text node**, so it renders.

The same happens with an empty string in some cases, and `NaN` renders as "NaN".

```jsx
// ✅ Compare explicitly
{cart.length > 0 && <CartSummary items={cart} />}

// ✅ Or coerce to boolean
{Boolean(cart.length) && <CartSummary items={cart} />}

// ✅ Or use a ternary with an explicit null
{cart.length ? <CartSummary items={cart} /> : null}
```

---

## What React skips vs renders

| Value | Rendered? |
|-------|-----------|
| `false`, `null`, `undefined` | Skipped |
| `0` | **Rendered as "0"** |
| `''` | Rendered as nothing (but still a text node) |
| `NaN` | **Rendered as "NaN"** |

---

## Readability rule

One ternary is fine. Nested ternaries in JSX are where code review goes wrong:

```jsx
// ❌ Unreadable
{loading ? <Spinner /> : error ? <Error /> : data ? <List /> : <Empty />}

// ✅ Early returns
if (loading) return <Spinner />;
if (error) return <Error error={error} />;
if (!data?.length) return <Empty />;
return <List data={data} />;
```

## The problem it solves

```jsx
// ❌ A function can only return one value
function Columns() {
  return (
    <td>Name</td>
    <td>Email</td>
  );
}

// ❌ A div here produces invalid HTML — the browser will move it
function Columns() {
  return <div><td>Name</td><td>Email</td></div>;
}

// ✅ Fragment groups without a DOM node
function Columns() {
  return (
    <>
      <td>Name</td>
      <td>Email</td>
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Two syntaxes

```jsx
// Short — the usual choice
<>
  <Header />
  <Content />
</>

// Long — required when you need a key
import { Fragment } from 'react';

{rows.map((row) => (
  <Fragment key={row.id}>
    <dt>{row.term}</dt>
    <dd>{row.definition}</dd>
  </Fragment>
))}
```

The short syntax accepts **no props at all** — no `key`, no `className`. A keyed list of paired elements is the main reason to reach for the long form.

---

## Why an extra div is not harmless

**CSS layout breaks.** Flex and grid only apply to *direct* children:

```jsx
// The div becomes the only grid item — the cards no longer lay out in the grid
<div className="grid grid-cols-3">
  <div>
    <Card /><Card /><Card />
  </div>
</div>
```

**Invalid HTML.** `<table>` expects `<tbody>`, `<ul>` expects `<li>`. A stray div gets relocated by the browser's parser, which then causes a hydration mismatch under SSR.

**Div soup.** Deeply nested wrappers make DevTools harder to read and add nodes to every render.

---

## Rule of thumb

Use a Fragment by default. Only use a `div` when you actually want to style or position that element.

## The three triggers

| Trigger | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Own state changed** | `useState` setter or `useReducer` dispatch with a *new* value |
| **Parent re-rendered** | Cascades down the whole subtree by default |
| **Consumed context changed** | Any `useContext` consumer, when the provider's `value` changes identity |

---

## The one people get wrong

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <ExpensiveChild />   {/* ← no props at all, still re-renders every click */}
    </>
  );
}
```

`ExpensiveChild` re-renders because its parent did. It receives nothing. Its output is identical. React re-runs it anyway.

This is usually fine — re-rendering is cheap, and React skips the DOM update when the output matches. It only matters when the component is genuinely expensive.

---

## Bailing out

```jsx
// Skips re-render when props are shallow-equal
const ExpensiveChild = memo(function ExpensiveChild({ data }) {
  return <Chart data={data} />;
});
```

Or, often better, restructure so the state lives closer to where it is used:

```jsx
// Children passed as JSX are created by the grandparent, so their
// element identity does not change when Parent's state changes
function Parent({ children }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>
      {children}   {/* ← does not re-render */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## When state does NOT trigger a re-render

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
setCount(0);          // Same value — React bails out (Object.is comparison)

const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Ada' });
user.name = 'Grace';  // ❌ Mutation — same reference, no re-render at all
setUser(user);        // ❌ Still the same reference. Nothing happens.
setUser({ ...user, name: 'Grace' });  // ✅ New reference — re-renders
```

> Mutating state and wondering why the screen did not update is the single most common React bug. React compares with `Object.is`, so a mutated object is indistinguishable from the old one.

## Controlled

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');

  return (
    <input
      value={name}                                  // React drives the value
      onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}     // every keystroke → state
    />
  );
}
```

State is the source of truth. The DOM is downstream of it.

---

## Uncontrolled

```jsx
function NameField() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log(inputRef.current.value);   // read it when you need it
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input ref={inputRef} defaultValue="" />   {/* note: defaultValue */}
    </form>
  );
}
```

The DOM holds the value. React only asks for it at submit time.

---

## Choosing

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Validate as the user types | Controlled |
| Disable submit until valid | Controlled |
| Format while typing (phone, currency) | Controlled |
| Field depends on another field | Controlled |
| Simple form, only read on submit | Uncontrolled |
| File input | Uncontrolled — `<input type="file">` is always uncontrolled |
| Large form with performance problems | Uncontrolled, or React Hook Form |

---

## The warning everyone hits

> *A component is changing an uncontrolled input to be controlled.*

Caused by initialising state as `undefined`:

```jsx
const [name, setName] = useState();          // ❌ undefined → uncontrolled
const [name, setName] = useState('');         // ✅ '' → controlled from the start

// Same problem from an async load
<input value={user?.name} />                 // ❌ undefined until user arrives
<input value={user?.name ?? ''} />           // ✅
```

---

## The middle ground

React Hook Form uses uncontrolled inputs under the hood and subscribes only where needed. You get validation without re-rendering the whole form on every keystroke — which is why it is the default choice for large forms.

## The basics

```jsx
function Card({ children }) {
  return <div className="card">{children}</div>;
}

<Card>
  <h2>Title</h2>
  <p>Body text</p>
</Card>
```

It really is just a prop — this is equivalent:

```jsx
<Card children={<h2>Title</h2>} />
```

---

## Multiple slots

When one slot is not enough, pass elements as named props. This is React's version of named slots:

```jsx
function Layout({ header, sidebar, children, footer }) {
  return (
    <div className="layout">
      <header>{header}</header>
      <aside>{sidebar}</aside>
      <main>{children}</main>
      <footer>{footer}</footer>
    </div>
  );
}

<Layout
  header={<Nav />}
  sidebar={<Filters />}
  footer={<Legal />}
>
  <ProductList />
</Layout>
```

---

## children as a function (render props)

```jsx
function MousePosition({ children }) {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });
  useEffect(() => {
    const onMove = (e) => setPos({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    window.addEventListener('mousemove', onMove);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMove);
  }, []);
  return children(pos);   // hand the state to the caller
}

<MousePosition>
  {({ x, y }) => <p>{x}, {y}</p>}
</MousePosition>
```

Hooks replaced most uses of this, but it is still the right tool when a component owns rendering *and* needs the caller to decide what to draw — virtualised lists and chart libraries use it constantly.

---

## The performance side effect

Children passed from a grandparent are created **outside** the parent, so the parent re-rendering does not re-render them:

```jsx
function Wrapper({ children }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  // Toggling `open` does NOT re-render children — the element
  // objects were created by whoever rendered <Wrapper>.
  return <div>{open && <Panel />}{children}</div>;
}
```

This is a free optimisation people often reach for `memo` to achieve.

---

## Manipulating children

`React.Children.map`, `.count` and `.toArray` exist for iterating over children safely, and `cloneElement` can inject props. Both are legacy-flavoured now — the docs recommend passing data through props or context instead, because cloning makes the data flow invisible.

## What synthetic events give you

1. **Normalised behaviour** across browsers — one API, no vendor quirks.
2. **Delegation** — React attaches listeners to the root container, not to every element. Ten thousand buttons still means one `click` listener.
3. **Consistent naming** — always camelCase: `onClick`, `onChange`, `onMouseEnter`.

```jsx
function Button() {
  return (
    <button onClick={(e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      console.log(e.type);          // 'click' — synthetic
      console.log(e.nativeEvent);   // the real browser event
    }}>
      Click
    </button>
  );
}
```

---

## Where React's behaviour differs from the DOM

**`onChange` fires on every keystroke.** The native DOM `change` event fires on blur. React's `onChange` behaves like the native `input` event — this is deliberate and catches people out coming from vanilla JS.

**Delegation target changed in React 17.** Listeners moved from `document` to the root container, which fixed a class of bugs when embedding React inside another app or running two React versions on one page.

**Event pooling is gone.** React 16 reused event objects, so reading `e.target` inside an async callback gave you `null` and required `e.persist()`. React 17 removed pooling — this is now only relevant for legacy code.

---

## Stopping propagation

```jsx
// Stops it within React's tree
onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}

// If a non-React listener on document also needs stopping
onClick={(e) => e.nativeEvent.stopImmediatePropagation()}
```

Mixing React handlers with `document.addEventListener` is where ordering surprises come from — the native listener on `document` may fire before or after React's, depending on where React's root sits.

---

## Capture phase

```jsx
<div onClickCapture={onCapture} onClick={onBubble}>
```

Suffix `Capture` to run during the capture phase instead of bubbling.

---

## When to skip synthetic events

For listeners on `window` or `document`, or when you need `{ passive: true }` for scroll performance, use a native listener in an effect:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const onScroll = () => { /* ... */ };
  window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
  return () => window.removeEventListener('scroll', onScroll);
}, []);
```

## The underlying reason

JSX compiles to a function call, and props must be values:

```jsx
<div>{something}</div>
// becomes
_jsx('div', { children: something })
//                       ↑ must be a value
```

You cannot pass an `if` statement as a function argument in any JavaScript context. JSX is no exception.

---

## Conditionals

```jsx
// ❌ Statements
{if (isAdmin) { <AdminPanel /> }}
{switch (status) { ... }}

// ✅ Expressions
{isAdmin ? <AdminPanel /> : null}
{isAdmin && <AdminPanel />}
{ { idle: <Idle />, busy: <Busy /> }[status] }
```

---

## Loops

```jsx
// ❌ for is a statement and returns nothing
{for (const item of items) { <li>{item.name}</li> }}

// ✅ map returns an array, and React renders arrays
{items.map((item) => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}
```

React renders arrays of elements natively — that is why `map` is the idiom and why each element needs a `key`.

---

## When the logic is too big for an expression

Move it above the `return`. This is almost always more readable than fighting to fit it inline:

```jsx
function Status({ state, error }) {
  let content;

  if (state === 'loading') {
    content = <Spinner />;
  } else if (state === 'error') {
    content = <Error message={error.message} />;
  } else {
    content = <Data />;
  }

  return <section>{content}</section>;
}
```

Or extract it into a function, which lets you use `switch` and early returns freely:

```jsx
function renderStatus(state) {
  switch (state) {
    case 'loading': return <Spinner />;
    case 'error':   return <Error />;
    default:        return <Data />;
  }
}

return <section>{renderStatus(state)}</section>;
```

---

## The one exception people notice

An IIFE technically works, because a function *call* is an expression:

```jsx
{(() => { if (a) return <A />; return <B />; })()}
```

It works and it is legal. It is also harder to read than any of the options above, so treat it as a curiosity rather than a pattern.
